Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Tangent Function Transformed by Multiple Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "c6fc00dc-8a19-54ee-958e-a8f91db106df" :question/math-academy-id "e-3789" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\tan\\left(2x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 2 = 0$ for $0\\leq x < 2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi.$ Notice that setting $n=0$ gives a result outside of the domain.\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}.$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}.$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\dfrac{23\\pi}{12}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi.$ So, the solutions $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}, \\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}, \\dfrac{23\\pi}{12} .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9eb12a99-5169-52f7-8830-5de0d2d854f6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing a Transformed Tangent Function Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "8bd9181d-6e46-59d7-bf1a-ea327c519b4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3794" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\tan\\left(3x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1$ for $-\\pi < x \\leq \\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi < x \\leq \\pi.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0.$\n\n- Setting $n=\\pm1$ gives $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n- Setting $n=\\pm2$ gives $x=\\pm\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\pi.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi < x \\leq \\pi.$ So, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = 0,\\, \\pm \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\, \\pm\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\, \\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f40f50e-49a7-5eda-b642-dc7085569b23" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Tangent Function Transformed by One Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4d4722-2cee-5fdc-a9c5-8fdbd157b21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "346" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279634} {:answer/id #uuid "7d21d76b-a2e2-5856-920e-79158a44a07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "328" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279635} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4f6f1f-e6b0-5df4-93d6-4ed188f077a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279636} {:answer/id #uuid "0db51c13-801d-5fe8-8975-57a7b7c83afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "374" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279637} {:answer/id #uuid "6280b1cf-f281-51b8-a12c-b2e37a73d5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6280b1cf-f281-51b8-a12c-b2e37a73d5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279638} :answer-field/id #uuid "771ec446-9140-5989-bc29-50d1bbb9e6f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8b142f5-ce7a-5298-95ab-983cc19acd14" :question/math-academy-id "q-121557" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})=-1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{4} & =-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =-{180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-{36}^{∘}+n\\cdot {144}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={108}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={252}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions are $x={108}^{∘},{252}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ is $108+252=360.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9eb12a99-5169-52f7-8830-5de0d2d854f6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing a Transformed Tangent Function Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7718bc86-72da-5c01-b4ef-3856325f575c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279641} {:answer/id #uuid "9a30c90e-f891-5c42-b439-f9b6277aa206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279642} {:answer/id #uuid "8a78c5ab-dedb-5d05-a9be-2cba49c14588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279643} {:answer/id #uuid "39706848-34cb-52b6-a137-adfe0a1da07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279644} {:answer/id #uuid "9abeff4f-7081-5c4e-93bc-7416794b815a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39706848-34cb-52b6-a137-adfe0a1da07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279644} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a90c4ce-ffc2-5c3e-ae6e-f9123deb070d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "928a8e32-c255-52c7-a2c8-64bff84ba192" :question/math-academy-id "q-121558" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+6n)\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{18}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{18}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{18}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{18},\\frac{7\\pi }{18},\\frac{13\\pi }{18}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{13\\pi }{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3bd5e5c-b8d1-53e5-9968-8bef8faceb4b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Tangent Function Transformed by Multiple Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90da44e1-f12b-5dfd-a0cb-0a1ca57f017b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279648} {:answer/id #uuid "8f32aee0-2a31-5231-875c-eda816c432d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279649} {:answer/id #uuid "7533ba62-b7b6-54cb-b91c-e375dea2d9c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279650} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d3c97c-13bb-5bec-a61f-3a5788ffeb9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279651} {:answer/id #uuid "31784df0-4201-5545-a910-2a76cd6fe250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f32aee0-2a31-5231-875c-eda816c432d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279649} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd95be2f-782d-5c9d-b699-586d1c627f61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a2e563d-e079-592c-b11c-ba8b756c914d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124345" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+6n)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{19\\pi }{12}=\\frac{20\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f40f50e-49a7-5eda-b642-dc7085569b23" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Tangent Function Transformed by One Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d4d69ff-cbd4-5ad3-9984-f9be4f9f1151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279655} {:answer/id #uuid "7b04c5fc-1efe-5d6b-9ff8-953fa6c02318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b04c5fc-1efe-5d6b-9ff8-953fa6c02318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279656} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec41c19-5cd5-53c3-b05a-fb7029122057" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279654} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db79371a-925a-59b1-8a7d-f843a6eab460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db79371a-925a-59b1-8a7d-f843a6eab460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279658} :answer-field/id #uuid "37df39df-2d11-5842-8d93-21bf072d84c6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279657} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1c59ca3-cf24-59f0-a182-75ade4278eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1c59ca3-cf24-59f0-a182-75ade4278eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279660} :answer-field/id #uuid "914f95e7-c35b-5743-9ed7-0da3b71b9dac" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3cda6184-daeb-5e3b-a514-5955a66ce241" :question/math-academy-id "q-274622" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the **exact solutions** to the equation $4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3})+4=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3})+4 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3}) & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{3} & =-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{27}^{∘}+n\\cdot {108}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={81}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={189}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={297}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={81}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={189}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={297}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f40f50e-49a7-5e